## What it does

HailTrigger turns public property records and hail and wind data into a ranked list of homes worth contacting after a storm. Contractors choose homes for direct-mail campaigns, every mailer carries its own QR code, and scans, bookings and leads are attributed back to each address.

It serves two very different users: contractor staff working in a dense dashboard, and homeowners scanning a postcard on their phone after a storm. It's positioned as targeting intelligence, never a damage claim.

## Built with agents

HailTrigger was built by a two-person team working with large numbers of parallel AI coding agents. By August 2026 the live product had 4,071 commits, 2,371 pull requests and 529 database migrations. A ground-up rebuild is underway on a stricter kernel-and-modules architecture.

## The roof model

To estimate roof vulnerability, I trained a roof-material classifier on authorized county aerial imagery, first fine-tuning a ResNet-50 and then training a classification head on DINOv2 features. The v4 model reached 86.7% exact-material accuracy on a 372-property holdout, and 92.2% on the 85% of properties where it was at least 95% confident. It runs as a canary, not yet the default.

Built with Next.js, Postgres/PostGIS, a Python worker, MapLibre and print-mail APIs.

